---
import DeveloperPage from '../../components/DeveloperPage.astro';
import csharp from '../../developers/samples/ScreenHireSignature.cs?raw';
import node from '../../developers/samples/verify-signature.mjs?raw';
import vector from '../../developers/samples/signature-vector.json';
import python from '../../developers/samples/verify_signature.py?raw';
import { inlineCode, webhookEvents } from '../../lib/developers';
import { pages } from '../../lib/pages';

const events = webhookEvents();
// The tested vector's header (SignatureSampleTests, developers.test.ts), so the example on the page is one the API signs.
const header = `ScreenHire-Signature: ${vector.header}`;
---

<DeveloperPage
  page={pages.developerWebhooks}
  heading="Webhooks"
  lead="ScreenHire sends a signed POST to your endpoint when something happens. Each event carries ids and facts, never a candidate's name, email or answers."
>
  <h2>Add an endpoint</h2>
  <p>
    An Owner or Admin adds an endpoint in Settings, Webhooks: an HTTPS address and the events it wants. Its signing secret
    (<code>shws_...</code>) is shown once and can be rotated. To read more than the event says, call the{' '}
    <a href={pages.developerApi.path}>API</a> with the ids it carries.
  </p>

  <h2>Every delivery</h2>
  <p>
    The body is JSON with the same five fields for every event: <code>id</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>occurredAt</code>,{' '}
    <code>organizationId</code> and <code>data</code>, which differs by event. These headers come with it:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>ScreenHire-Signature</code>: when it was sent and the signature, as below.</li>
    <li><code>ScreenHire-Event-Id</code> and <code>Idempotency-Key</code>: the event's id, the same on every attempt and replay.</li>
    <li><code>ScreenHire-Event-Type</code>: the event's type, and <code>ScreenHire-Delivery-Id</code>: this delivery.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Check the signature</h2>
  <p>
    The signature header looks like this. <code>v1</code> is the hex HMAC-SHA256 of the time, a full stop and the raw body,
    keyed with your endpoint's secret:
  </p>
  <pre tabindex="0"><code>{header}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Check it on the body exactly as it arrived, before you parse it, and refuse a <code>t</code> more than five minutes old so a
    captured delivery cannot be sent again.
  </p>
  <h3>Node</h3>
  <pre tabindex="0"><code>{node}</code></pre>
  <h3>Python</h3>
  <pre tabindex="0"><code>{python}</code></pre>
  <h3>C#</h3>
  <pre tabindex="0"><code>{csharp}</code></pre>

  <h2>Retries and replays</h2>
  <p>
    Answer with any 2xx status to say you have it. Anything else, or no answer in time, is tried again after 1 minute, 5 minutes,
    30 minutes, 2 hours, 6 hours and 12 hours: seven attempts over about 21 hours. The same event can arrive more than once, so
    use its <code>id</code> to ignore one you have handled. After five deliveries in a row fail every attempt, the endpoint is
    turned off and Settings says why; turn it on again there. Each endpoint's delivery log is kept for 30 days, and any delivery
    can be sent again from it with the same event id.
  </p>

  <h2>Events</h2>
  {
    events.map((event) => (
      <>
        <h3 id={event.type}>
          <code>{event.type}</code>
        </h3>
        <p>
          <strong>{event.summary}.</strong> {inlineCode(event.description).map((part) => (part.code ? <code>{part.text}</code> : part.text))}
        </p>
        <pre tabindex="0"><code>{event.sample}</code></pre>
      </>
    ))
  }
</DeveloperPage>
